`__instN` is one sequence, and two passes draw from it.

A substituted component's state is renamed with a suffix so two call sites do
not share a cell. Which pass does the substituting depends on what the component
is: `passInlinePure` takes a platform override, because every platform-package
component must inline or the build fails, and `passNoInlineComponents` takes an
ordinary user component. Both emit into one host namespace, and each used to
hold a counter of its own starting at zero -- so an owner holding one of each
declared two `hits__inst0`. Kotlin and Go both refuse that; html shadowed one
key of its `state` object with the other and lost a counter in silence.

`gauge` is the first kind and `tally` the second, instantiated side by side in
one window, which is the smallest program that asks both passes for a suffix.
A *double* suffix, by contrast, is not the symptom and is correct wherever it
appears: one var renamed twice as it travels through two owners.

Which target shows it turns on something unrelated:
`passInlinePure` substitutes an override whose var nothing *rendered* reads, and
android lowers `@click` into a `Button(onClick = …)` prop before the question is
asked -- so android reads the handler as a rendered read, calls the override
impure, and hands both components to the one pass. html keeps the handler a
handler, calls the override pure, and asks both passes. One source file, two
verdicts, and the collision follows the verdict rather than the program.

The three Go targets render `gauge`'s base body, which holds nothing, so they
ask one pass for one suffix. They are here to say that sharing the counter moved
nothing for a build that was never going to collide.

deny out/none/html/index.html `(?s)hits__inst0: 0.*hits__inst0: 0` -- the page's state object keyed twice by one name keeps one of the two counters and drops the other; esbuild warns, the build does not fail

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import "sngl:platform/android"
import "sngl:platform/bubbletea"
import "sngl:platform/fyne"
import "sngl:platform/gtk4"
import "sngl:platform/html"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
}

component gauge(label = "") node {
    text(value="base {label}")
}

component gauge[android.platform] {
    var hits = 0

    button(text=label, @click {
        hits = hits + 1
    })
}

component gauge[html.platform] {
    var hits = 0

    button(text=label, @click {
        hits = hits + 1
    })
}

component tally(label = "") node {
    var hits = 0

    button(text="{label} {hits}", @click {
        hits = hits + 1
    })
}

window {
    vbox {
        gauge(label="g")
        tally(label="t")
    }
}
